Q: Why does std::vector::push_back destructs pushed object? And how to push an object without creating a temporary variable? A: <blockquote>
<p>When pushing the newly constructed object into vector, it seems to destruct it twice: first in push_back, second - in the vector's own destructor. Why does this happen?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Your code is not destructing the same object twice.  It is actually destructing two separate objects. You are logging the destruction of both objects, but you are not logging the construction of both objects, only one of them.</p>
<p>You are creating a <em>temporary</em> object, and then pushing a <em>copy</em> into the <code>std::vector</code>.  You are logging in <code>A</code>'s <strong>converting constructor</strong>, but you are not logging anything in its <strong>copy constructor</strong>.  You should add that logging.  You should also log out the value of the <code>this</code> pointer as well, so you can see which calls belong to which object.</p>
<p>Try this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;

using namespace std;

class a {
    public:
        int m_a,m_b;

        a(int _a, int _b):
            m_a {_a},
            m_b {_b} {
            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;init a: &quot; &lt;&lt; this &lt;&lt; endl;
        }

        // add this
        a(const a &amp;src) :
            m_a {src.m_a},
            m_b {src.m_b} {
            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;copy a: &quot; &lt;&lt; this &lt;&lt; &quot; &lt;- &quot; &lt;&lt; &amp;src &lt;&lt; endl;
        }

        ~a() {
            cout &lt;&lt; &quot;destroy a: &quot; &lt;&lt; this &lt;&lt; endl;
        }
};

int main(void) {
    vector&lt;a&gt; b;
    b.push_back(a {1, 2});
}
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>init a: 0x7ffdb8dd0b28
copy a: 0x58cc12eb3d30 &lt;- 0x7ffdb8dd0b28
destroy a: 0x7ffdb8dd0b28
destroy a: 0x58cc12eb3d30
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://onlinegdb.com/FsgwABPmI" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Online Demo</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>In non-example where I'm trying to use std::vector, this behaviour segfaults the program during the vector's destructor.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That would imply that you have most likely violated the <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/rule_of_three.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Rule of 3</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If a class requires a user-defined destructor, a user-defined copy constructor, or a user-defined copy assignment operator, it almost certainly requires all three.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If your class owns a resource, but does not handle copying/moving of that resource properly, then you can end up in situations where multiple object instances of your class may own the same resource, hence the segfault when one object frees the resource and then another object tries to use/free that same resource.</p>
<p>If your class owns a resource, make sure you properly implement copy semantics (Rule of 3) and move semantics (Rule of 5) for it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Can the temporary object created by a {1, 2} or analogues be avoided? In other words: can std::vector::push_back construct object from general constructor with more than one argument?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes. You can use <code>emplace_back()</code> instead of <code>push_back()</code>, eg:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>b.emplace_back(1, 2);
</code></pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>init a: 0x55db5cde7920
destroy a: 0x55db5cde7920
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://onlinegdb.com/ZLOXZJ-yR" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Online Demo</a></p>

Q: How can I connect to MongoDB using Express.js without Mongoose? A: <p>Use:</p>
<pre><code>var mongodb = require('mongodb');
var uri = 'mongodb://localhost:27017/dbname';

module.exports = function(callback) {
  mongodb.MongoClient.connect(uri, callback);
};
</code></pre>
<p>Add this snippet in a file, say, <em>connect.js</em>, and then require this file (<em>connect.js</em>) in your file where you are declaring your functions for HTTP requests.</p>

Q: Trying to update an array element by mapping user input (via key/value mapping). The array element in square &#39;1&#39; should now be a &#39;x&#39; as mapped value? A: <p>Map and the array store separate copies of the strings. You need to use references instead:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;map&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;functional&gt;   // std::reference_wrapper, std::ref
#include &lt;string&gt;

using namespace std;

int main()
{
    int user_input;

    // create board positions
    string board_array[3][3] =
    {
        {&quot;1&quot;,&quot;2&quot;,&quot;3&quot;},
        {&quot;4&quot;,&quot;5&quot;,&quot;6&quot;},
        {&quot;7&quot;,&quot;8&quot;,&quot;9&quot;}
    };

    // map squares to references to the actual array elements
    map&lt;int, reference_wrapper&lt;string&gt;&gt; mapped_square;
    int n = 1;
    for (int r = 0; r &lt; 3; r++)
    {
        for (int c = 0; c &lt; 3; c++)
        {
            mapped_square.emplace(n, ref(board_array[r][c]));
            n++;
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;What square do you choose? &quot; &lt;&lt; endl;
    cin &gt;&gt; user_input;

    auto it = mapped_square.find(user_input);
    if (it != mapped_square.end())
    {
        it-&gt;second.get() = &quot;x&quot;;  // updates board_array too
    }
    else
    {
        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Invalid square.\n&quot;;
    }

    // Display board
    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;+-----------------+&quot; &lt;&lt; endl
         &lt;&lt; &quot;|  &quot; &lt;&lt; board_array[0][0] &lt;&lt; &quot;  |  &quot; &lt;&lt; board_array[0][1] &lt;&lt; &quot;  |  &quot; &lt;&lt; board_array[0][2] &lt;&lt; &quot;  |&quot; &lt;&lt; endl
         &lt;&lt; &quot;|-----------------|&quot; &lt;&lt; endl
         &lt;&lt; &quot;|  &quot; &lt;&lt; board_array[1][0] &lt;&lt; &quot;  |  &quot; &lt;&lt; board_array[1][1] &lt;&lt; &quot;  |  &quot; &lt;&lt; board_array[1][2] &lt;&lt; &quot;  |&quot; &lt;&lt; endl
         &lt;&lt; &quot;|-----------------|&quot; &lt;&lt; endl
         &lt;&lt; &quot;|  &quot; &lt;&lt; board_array[2][0] &lt;&lt; &quot;  |  &quot; &lt;&lt; board_array[2][1] &lt;&lt; &quot;  |  &quot; &lt;&lt; board_array[2][2] &lt;&lt; &quot;  |&quot; &lt;&lt; endl
         &lt;&lt; &quot;+-----------------+&quot; &lt;&lt; endl;

    return 0;
}

</code></pre>
